  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">PRCS/L Health in Crisis Phase IV</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="2">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">In recent years with support from MoFA, NLRC has been able to work together with PRCS/L on strengthening their Response Preparedness and on supporting PRCS/L&#x2019;s hospitals and the capacity building of their staff and volunteers under the successive Health in Crisis I, II and III projects. The proposed Health in Crisis IV project aims to continue NLRC&#x2019;s support to the core services of PRCS/L, as well as to complement that of other RCRC Movement partners, in ensuring the provision of medicines, medical supplies and equipment to the five hospitals of PRCS/L and coverage of hospital running costs and deficits in in-patient bills.

As PRCS/L further develops its capacity for health emergency response, particularly at the community level and in pre-hospital care during times of crisis, the project will also continue supporting PRCS/L in institutional resilience building, and the implementation of the PRCS/L Health Emergency Response Framework (supported by NLRC through the Response Preparedness II project), and to ensure that the newly established PRCS/L response structures (supported by the Response Preparedness II and MADAD projects) from the community to the hospital level are meeting the needs of affected populations during emergencies and disasters.

A new component included in the Health in Crisis IV project is focused on identifying renewable energy solutions, aimed at making the provision of PRCS/L&#x2019;s life-saving services more resilient and sustainable. Currently the national power grid in Lebanon is supplying a maximum of two hours of electricity per day, meaning that for the remaining hours people are dependent on fuel-powered generators. At the same time, the lifting of government subsidies, have led to a 66% increase in fuel prizes in 2021, which has been further exacerbated by the effects of the conflict in Ukraine. Supporting PRCS/L with the identification and implementation of renewable energy solutions, will help to ensure the continuous provision of their life-saving services in a more sustainable and reliable way, while at the same time saving money on costly fuel needed to run generators.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Lebanon</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">PRCS/L Health in Crisis Phase IV</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Since 2019 Lebanon has been hit by crisis after crisis, with the COVID-19 pandemic, the Beirut port explosions and an ongoing economic crisis. The nationwide economic collapse and a continuing political freeze have a huge impact on the whole population and especially refugees. Due to the protracted crisis situation, PRCS/L has to respond to an increased demand on their life-saving services.</narrative>
  </description>
